  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "These topics are specific to <strong>Analysis and Approaches HL</strong>.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Convergence of series",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\[\\text{Geometric series converges iff }|r|&lt;1:\\quad S_\\infty=\\frac{a}{1-r}.\\]\\[\\text{Ratio test: if }\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right|&lt;1\\Rightarrow\\text{series converges absolutely}.\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Graphs of inverse trig functions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\[\\sin^{-1}:\\;[-1,1]\\to[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2],\\quad\\cos^{-1}:\\;[-1,1]\\to[0,\\pi],\\quad\\tan^{-1}:\\;\\mathbb{R}\\to(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2).\\]\\[\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin^{-1}x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}},\\quad\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos^{-1}x)=\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}},\\quad\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1}x)=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}.\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Inverse trig functions and their derivatives",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "ibhlInvTrigCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "The graph of an inverse trig function and its derivative, with a movable point showing both values at once",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "ibhlInvTrigDataBtn",
          "panelId": "ibhlInvTrigDataPanel",
          "descId": "ibhlInvTrigDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "ibhlInvTrigDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "Sampled points on the chosen function and its derivative",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "x", "ar": null },
            { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null },
            { "en": "f′(x)", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "ibhlInvTrigFn",
            "label": { "en": "function: ", "ar": null },
            "value": "arcsin",
            "options": [
              { "value": "arcsin", "label": "sin⁻¹ x", "selected": true },
              { "value": "arccos", "label": "cos⁻¹ x" },
              { "value": "arctan", "label": "tan⁻¹ x" }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ibhlInvTrigA",
            "label": { "en": "<b>x</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -98,
            "max": 98,
            "value": 50,
            "step": 2,
            "valueDisplayId": "ibhlInvTrigAVal",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "x value (as a percentage of the domain slider range)", "ar": null }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "ibhlInvTrigFA",
            "label": { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "indigo",
            "value": "0.524"
          },
          {
            "id": "ibhlInvTrigDA",
            "label": { "en": "f′(x)", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "1.155"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "Blue is the chosen inverse trig function; amber dashed is its derivative, from the formulas above. sin⁻¹ and cos⁻¹ are only defined on [−1, 1], so their derivative curves diverge near the ends of that interval (division by √(1−x²) → 0) — sin⁻¹'s derivative shoots up toward +∞ at both ends (it's 1/√(1−x²), always positive), while cos⁻¹'s shoots down toward −∞ instead (it's the negative of the same expression) — switch to tan⁻¹ to see a derivative that stays bounded everywhere instead, since 1/(1+x²) never blows up.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "workedExampleCard",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked Example — Limit and convergence",
          "ar": null
        },
        "problem": {
          "en": "Determine if \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n}{2^n}\\) converges.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>Ratio test: \\(\\left|\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right|=\\dfrac{n+1}{2^{n+1}}\\cdot\\dfrac{2^n}{n}=\\dfrac{n+1}{2n}\\to\\dfrac{1}{2}&lt;1.\\) Series <strong>converges</strong>.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Mathematical induction",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Base case; assume \\( n=k \\); prove \\( n=k+1 \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "De Moivre's theorem",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( (\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^n=\\cos n\\theta+i\\sin n\\theta \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Euler's form",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( e^{i\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "Practice Problem",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Find the Maclaurin series for \\(\\ln(1+2x)\\) up to the \\(x^3\\) term.</p><details style=\"margin-top:8px\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;font-weight:600\">Show solution</summary><div style=\"margin-top:8px\"><p>Use \\(\\ln(1+u)=u-\\frac{u^2}{2}+\\frac{u^3}{3}-\\cdots\\) with \\(u=2x\\):</p><p>\\(\\ln(1+2x)=2x-2x^2+\\frac{8x^3}{3}-\\cdots\\quad(-\\tfrac{1}{2}&lt;x\\le\\tfrac{1}{2}).\\)</p></div></details>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example-grid",
        "label": {
          "en": "Partial fractions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Decompose \\(\\dfrac{5x+1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\) into partial fractions.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> \\(\\dfrac{A}{x+1}+\\dfrac{B}{x-2}\\). Multiply: \\(5x+1=A(x-2)+B(x+1)\\).</p><p>\\(x=2\\): \\(11=3B\\implies B=\\frac{11}{3}\\). \\(x=-1\\): \\(-4=-3A\\implies A=\\frac{4}{3}\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "<strong>Practice:</strong> Prove that \\(\\cos 3\\theta = 4\\cos^3\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta\\) using De Moivre's theorem.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\((\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^3=\\cos3\\theta+i\\sin3\\theta\\). Expand left side using binomial; equate real parts.",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
